WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.710
While casting key roles in high-profile films,
it seems like Hollywood looks at every star

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:10.700
in the industry, tossing a half-dozen names
into the rumor mill before finally making

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:11.240
up their minds.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:17.200
These are roles, however, that were destined
for one actor and one actor only — and it's

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:18.320
easy to see why.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:20.260
Rubeus Hagrid

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:26.240
Because of the "U.K. only" casting policy
mandated by Harry Potter author J.K.

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:31.289
Rowling, several American actors who were
keen to get in on the Hogwarts action were

00:00:31.289 --> 00:00:36.640
turned away without so much as an audition,
including the legendary Robin Williams, who

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:39.850
had his eye on the role of groundskeeper Rubeus
Hagrid.

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:44.989
But Rowling herself only ever envisioned one
man for the role: veteran character actor

00:00:44.989 --> 00:00:45.740
Robbie Coltrane.

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:47.340
"Who are you?"

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:49.100
"Rubeus Hagrid.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:52.280
Keeper of keys and grounds at Hogwarts."

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:58.020
It's tough to imagine anyone else in the role
of the gentle half-giant, based on a Hell's

00:00:58.020 --> 00:00:59.700
Angel biker Rowling once knew.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:05.580
Fans will recall that Hagrid's first appearance
is aboard a flying motorcycle, which now makes

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:06.460
a bit more sense.

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:08.100
Hellboy

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:14.619
For most of his career, Ron Perlman was known
as a talented actor who almost always appeared

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:16.900
under layers of makeup and prosthetics.

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:23.100
His first film role was as a neanderthal in
1981's Quest for Fire, and his breakout gig

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:28.460
came in 1987, starring opposite Linda Hamilton
in the TV series Beauty and the Beast.

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:34.039
So it makes sense Guillermo Del Toro favored
him for the lead role in his 2004 adaptation

00:01:34.039 --> 00:01:36.700
of the Dark Horse comics series Hellboy.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:41.729
So intent was Del Toro on casting Perlman
in the title role that he turned down a bigger

00:01:41.729 --> 00:01:47.120
budget, a bevy of A-list actors, and two studios
in his quest to get the film made.

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:52.939
In particular, Universal virtually forced
Del Toro to talk with multiple major stars

00:01:52.939 --> 00:01:58.310
about the role for years, only for the determined
Del Toro to repeat his mantra to executives

00:01:58.310 --> 00:02:02.800
after every meeting: "Yeah, I like the guy…
but I like Ron Perlman more."

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:04.200
Nada

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:11.180
John Carpenter's They Live is a dystopian
sci-fi B-movie masterpiece with a protagonist

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:15.140
never referred to by name in the film — but
the credits call him "Nada."

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:21.660
Nada is a stone-faced homeless drifter who
discovers, via magic sunglasses, that aliens

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:26.530
are living in our midst, controlling all of
society, and he deals with this information

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:29.240
in his own...um... unique way …

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:33.200
"I have come here to chew bubblegum and kick
ass ... "

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:38.960
"And I'm all out of bubblegum."

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:45.240
It's a role Carpenter's fans would have expected
to see Kurt Russell in, and practically nobody

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:48.580
else, but Carpenter's longtime muse wasn't
even considered.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:53.820
Nobody was, except a professional wrestler
with a rather limited acting resume.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:59.360
"Rowdy" Roddy Piper brought depth, low-key
charm, and a rugged everyman presence to the

00:02:59.360 --> 00:02:59.860
role.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:05.400
Carpenter says Piper was, quote, "just absolutely
perfect, so I never thought of anybody else."

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:07.280
Nick Fury

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:14.500
In 2002, Marvel Comics artists Mark Millar
was asked to redesign some classic characters

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:16.260
for Marvel's Ultimate line.

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:21.680
One of these was S.H.I.E.L.D. director Nick
Fury, the eyepatch-sporting superspy who had

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:26.220
previously been portrayed by David Hasselhoff
in a little-remembered TV movie.

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:32.600
Millar envisioned his version of Fury as an
update of the character's cool '60s spy aesthetic,

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:38.520
and—deciding the name had a blaxploitation
bent to it—decided to cast his Fury in the

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:42.000
image of "the coolest man alive," Samuel L.
Jackson.

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:48.040
Unbeknownst to Marvel's writers, Jackson was
a huge comics fan and was fully aware of the

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:49.460
homage from the beginning.

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:55.300
The update was well-received by fans, and
Marvel Studios overlord Kevin Feige made an

00:03:55.300 --> 00:04:00.860
on-the-fly decision to cast Jackson as Fury
in Iron Man's famous post-credits stinger—a

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:03.000
scene that wasn't even in the original script.

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:08.940
The rest is history, as Fury became the glue
that holds the Marvel Cinematic Universe together

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:13.080
and the latest in a long series of iconic
roles for the beloved actor.

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:15.360
Drax the Destroyer

00:04:15.670 --> 00:04:21.180
Former wrestler Dave Bautista is pitch-perfect
as the - "not understanding what an idiom

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:25.740
is" - juggernaut Drax the Destroyer in Marvel's
Guardians of the Galaxy series.

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:29.290
"His people are completely literal, metaphors
are going to go over his head.

00:04:29.290 --> 00:04:31.130
"Nothing goes over my head.

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:33.030
My reflexes are too fast.

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:34.550
I would catch it."

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:39.040
There were persistent rumors that the role
was first offered to Jason Momoa, who then

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:43.180
flipped franchises to portray Aquaman in DC's
Extended Universe.

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:48.840
While Momoa confirms that he did audition
for the role of Drax, he says he removed himself

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:53.920
from consideration before an offer was made,
and that talks never got as far as fans speculated.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:57.520
Visiting the set of the first film in 2013,
Yahoo!

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:02.680
Movies put the final nail in the rumor's coffin,
confirming with cast and crew that Bautista,

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:06.930
while not hand-picked from the start, was
indeed the one and only actor ever offered

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:07.430
the role.

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:09.540
The Singular David Bowie

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:16.120
Generally considered to be the better of 2006's
two "magicians at the turn of the century"

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:21.220
films, Christopher Nolan's The Prestige is
a solid thriller about the lengths to which

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:24.880
two old-timey magicians would go in order
to screw with each other.

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:30.510
Hugh Jackman and Christian Bale headed up
an excellent cast, which included an absolutely

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:37.000
inspired choice to play insane genius Nikola
Tesla: David Bowie, the Thin White Duke himself.

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:42.680
The late Bowie had a famously otherworldly
presence, and Nolan never considered anybody

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:44.580
else to portray the enigmatic inventor.

00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:50.440
Almost twenty years prior, Bowie was also
Martin Scorsese's first and only choice for

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:55.140
the icy Pontius Pilate in 1988's The Last
Temptation of Christ.

00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:59.720
His performance was singled out among critics
as being more appropriate to the material

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:05.350
than that of his co-stars, proving Scorsese
understood that casting is much the same as

00:06:05.350 --> 00:06:09.840
life—when you've gotta have David Bowie,
you've gotta have David Bowie.

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:11.480
"Go home.

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:12.880
Forget this thing.

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:14.620
I can recognize an obsession.

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:16.620
No good will come of it."

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:18.070
Thanks for watching!

00:06:18.070 --> 00:06:20.770
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:23.090
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

